# Worked Example: GTM Plan for a Fictional Company

> **This is a fictional example for illustration only.** The company, numbers and results are invented to show how the templates are filled in.

**Company:** BrightLedger, a small accounting-software tool for independent clinics.
**Goal:** Win the first 25 paying clinics in 90 days.

## Stage 1: Research

| Attribute | Answer |
|---|---|
| Customer type | B2B |
| Segment | Independent clinics with 2 to 10 staff |
| Decision maker | Clinic owner or practice manager |
| Top problems | Manual billing, late payments, unclear monthly profit |
| Current solution | Spreadsheets and a part-time bookkeeper |
| Buying trigger | Year-end tax preparation, hiring a new doctor |

## Stage 2: Positioning

> **For** independent clinic owners **who** lose time on billing and bookkeeping, **BrightLedger** is a simple accounting tool **that** shows monthly profit in one screen. **Unlike** general accounting packages, **we** are built around clinic billing.

## Stage 3: Pricing and channels

| Item | Decision |
|---|---|
| Pricing | Tiered monthly plans (Starter, Standard, Plus) |
| Channel 1 | Direct outreach to clinic owners |
| Channel 2 | Partnerships with clinic equipment suppliers |
| Channel 3 | Referrals from existing customers |

## Stage 4: Launch plan (summary)

| Week | Action |
|---|---|
| 1 to 2 | Build the one-pager and demo; sign up 5 pilot clinics |
| 3 to 4 | Collect feedback; fix top three issues |
| 5 | Public launch with supplier partners |
| 6 to 12 | Weekly outreach and demos; monthly review |

## Stage 5: Scorecard (example 90-day targets)

| Metric | Target |
|---|---|
| Qualified leads | 150 |
| Demos | 60 |
| Paying clinics | 25 |
| Cost per customer | Below one month of subscription revenue |

## What the team learned (example)

- Clinics responded best to demos that showed their own billing data.
- The supplier channel produced fewer leads than expected but better-quality ones.
- Pricing tiers confused buyers, so the team reduced them to two.

*Use this example as a guide, then fill in the templates for your own business.*

# Frequently Asked Questions

**What is a GTM plan?**
A practical plan that explains who you sell to, what you offer, how you price it, how you reach customers and how you measure success.

**How long should it take to write one?**
A first version can be drafted in one to two weeks. Treat it as a living document and update it as you learn.

**Do I need all nine stages?**
No. Early-stage teams can start with Stages 1 to 5. Use Stages 6 to 8 when you enter new markets, build sales teams or work with partners.

**What if I have no customers yet?**
Start with Stage 1 and talk to at least ten potential customers before you finalise your positioning.

**How do I choose between channels?**
Score them using the table in Stage 3, then test the top two or three with small budgets.

**How often should I review the plan?**
Weekly for numbers, monthly for adjustments, quarterly for the whole plan.

**Can I use these templates for my business?**
Yes. This material is released under CC BY 4.0. Please credit the author.

**Is this legal or financial advice?**
No. It is a general framework. Consult qualified advisers for legal, tax and financial decisions.

# GTM Glossary

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **GTM (Go-to-Market)** | The plan for how a company brings an offer to customers and wins them |
| **ICP (Ideal Customer Profile)** | The type of customer who gets the most value from your offer and is most likely to buy |
| **Persona** | A semi-fictional description of a person in the buying process |
| **TAM / SAM / SOM** | Total, serviceable and obtainable market sizes |
| **Positioning** | How your offer is placed in the customer's mind relative to alternatives |
| **Value proposition** | The specific benefit you promise and why it matters to the customer |
| **Differentiator** | What you offer that competitors do not, or do much better |
| **Channel** | A route to reach customers, such as direct sales, partners or online |
| **Distributor** | A partner who holds stock and sells onward |
| **Lead** | A person or company showing interest |
| **Qualified lead** | A lead that fits your ICP and shows real buying interest |
| **Pipeline** | All active opportunities, grouped by sales stage |
| **Conversion rate** | The share of people who move from one stage to the next |
| **Sales cycle** | The time from first contact to closed deal |
| **CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost)** | The cost of winning one new customer |
| **LTV (Lifetime Value)** | The total revenue expected from a customer over time |
| **Churn** | The share of customers who stop buying in a period |
| **Battlecard** | A short guide that helps a salesperson compete against a specific rival |
